<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/advanced/sustainability-metrics-and-lca">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    LCA is <strong>accounting for environmental flows</strong> — the discipline that turns
    "eco-friendly" from marketing into measurement. Its rigour lives in the boundaries, the
    inventory data, and the honest handling of trade-offs between impacts. The syllabus's
    natural-resources unit (1.3–1.5) asks for sustainable management of minerals, energy
    and the conservation of both; LCA is the measurement layer that makes "sustainable"
    checkable — and the sustainability-metrics half of this lesson extends it from products
    to companies (scope 1–2–3 accounting), nations (footprints, decoupling) and the planet
    (planetary boundaries). The unifying principle, worth stating before any method:
    <strong>measurement precedes management</strong>. Every serious environmental target — a
    net-zero pledge, a circularity rate, a water budget — is only as real as the metric
    beneath it, and this page is about building metrics that survive hostile scrutiny.
  </p>
  <p>
    The four-phase architecture is standardised (ISO 14040/14044): <strong>goal and
    scope</strong> (what question, what boundaries, what <em>functional unit</em> — the
    service being compared), <strong>inventory analysis</strong> (every flow of energy,
    material, water and emission across the life cycle), <strong>impact assessment</strong>
    (inventory lines characterised into categories like CO₂-e, acidification potential,
    water scarcity), and <strong>interpretation</strong> (sensitivity and uncertainty
    analysis — which assumptions actually move the answer). The functional unit is the
    discipline's secret weapon: comparisons are meaningless until the service is defined
    (painting a wall vs coating it for 20 years; a cup vs 1,000 served drinks), and most
    greenwashing dissolves the moment someone asks what denominator the claim is riding on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the inventory, the boundaries, the categories, the classic findings" widget="equationStep" config={lcaCfg} />

  <h2>Characterisation: turning emissions into a common currency</h2>
  <p>
    The inventory produces a sprawl — tonnes of CH₄, kg of SO₂, cubic metres of water.
    Impact assessment converts each into a category indicator using characterisation
    factors. The most-used is <strong>global warming potential</strong>:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the characterisation arithmetic, the functional-unit trap, and the finding that kills 'food miles'" widget="equationStep" config={gwpCfg} />

  <p>
    The GWP horizon choice (20 vs 100 years) is not pedantry: methane is strong but
    short-lived (residence time ~12 years — the geochemical-cycles lesson's arithmetic),
    so a 20-year frame multiplies its weight fourfold — the technical debate behind
    GWP* and livestock accounting. After characterisation comes the honest bit: a
    product rarely wins everywhere (paper beats plastic on biodegradability and
    toxicity but loses on carbon and water; aluminium beats steel on recycling energy
    but loses on primary production). Multi-criteria results are <em>reported</em>, not
    collapsed into a single score — single-score methods exist, but they embed a
    value judgment about weighting that most practitioners prefer to leave explicit.
  </p>

  <h2>The classic findings, and why they surprise</h2>
  <p>
    Reusable packaging is the canonical trade-off case: a <strong>cotton tote</strong>
    carries a heavy agricultural footprint (land, water, fertiliser) and breaks even
    with single-use plastic bags only after ~130+ reuses (the UK Environment Agency's
    figure — Danish studies using organic cotton push it into the thousands), while
    paper needs ~3–4 uses. A <strong>ceramic mug</strong> beats paper cups after
    roughly 20–100 washes depending on how it is washed (dishwasher load size matters
    — the use phase is doing the work). <strong>Electric vehicles</strong> start with
    a ~30–40% manufacturing deficit (batteries) and claw it back at the plug: on a
    coal-heavy grid the break-even is ~60,000–90,000 km; on a clean grid, ~20,000 —
    the answer is a function of the grid, which is why honest EV claims state the
    electricity mix. <strong>Food</strong> is the field's most consequential finding:
    across 38,000 farms, Poore and Nemecek (2018) showed what-you-eat dominates
    where-it-came-from (transport ~10% of food emissions), beef sits at ~60 kg
    CO₂-e/kg (with a 20–100 range driven by land-use change) against ~6 for chicken
    and ~1 for legumes — and switching beef for beans at one meal moves more carbon
    than a year of careful shopping choices. The meta-lesson of all four cases:
    <strong>the use phase and the system context dominate</strong>, which is exactly
    what boundary-drawing is designed to catch.
  </p>

  <h2>Metrics beyond products: scopes, footprints, decoupling</h2>
  <p>
    Scaled to organisations, the standard is the <strong>GHG Protocol's scope
    1–2–3</strong>: scope 1, direct emissions (the company's own stacks); scope 2,
    purchased energy; scope 3, everything up and down the value chain — usually
    <em>the largest</em> (often 70–90% of a product company's total) and the least
    controlled, which is why mandatory disclosure regimes (the EU's CSRD, carbon
    reporting rules spreading elsewhere) matter more than voluntary pledges. At
    national scale, footprints disaggregate the ledger: <strong>production-based
    carbon</strong> (what a country emits within its borders) versus
    <strong>consumption-based</strong> (what its residents cause, wherever emitted —
    the distinction that reveals offshored emissions, roughly 20–25% of global CO₂),
    plus water, nitrogen, and material footprints. The economy-level question is
    <strong>decoupling</strong>: can GDP grow while environmental pressure falls?
    The evidence is nuanced — 30+ countries show <em>absolute</em> decoupling of
    production emissions from GDP (the EU's emissions fell ~30% since 1990 while its
    economy grew), but globally the coupling persists and resource use has decoupled
    far less than carbon. Two structural caveats keep the champagne corked: the
    <strong>rebound effect</strong> (efficiency lowers cost, cost raises demand —
    Jevons noted it for coal in 1865, and it is measured in everything from lighting
    to computing) and consumption-based leakage (domestic gains partly achieved by
    importing the footprint).
  </p>

  <h2>The gauge panel: footprints and planetary boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Aggregate metrics come in families, each a scaled-up LCA with its own quirks.
    <strong>Water footprints</strong> split into blue (surface and ground), green (soil
    moisture) and grey (the dilution volume a pollutant needs) — and regionalisation
    is everything: a tonne of almonds is water-trivial in wet regions and severe in
    drought ones, which is why "litres per product" without a location is a broken
    metric. <strong>Nitrogen footprints</strong> count reactive-N losses along the
    same food chains the geochemical-cycles lesson mapped; <strong>material
    footprints</strong> follow ores, biomass and fossil feedstocks through trade.
    Above the products sits the <strong>planetary-boundaries</strong> gauge panel
    (Rockström 2009; Steffen 2015; quantified update 2023): nine Earth-system limits —
    climate change, biosphere integrity, land-system change, freshwater change,
    biogeochemical flows (N and P), ocean acidification, atmospheric aerosol loading,
    stratospheric ozone, novel entities. The 2023 assessment found <strong>six of
    nine crossed</strong> (all but ocean acidification, aerosols and ozone — ozone
    the one the Montreal Protocol pulled back). Two cautions keep the framework
    honest: the boundaries carry real uncertainties (novel entities is largely a
    judgement), and they are context gauges for the planet, not management targets
    for a firm — the translation layer between the two is exactly where this
    discipline's research frontier sits.
  </p>

  <h2>Energy and materials: EROI and the circularity gap</h2>
  <p>
    Two aggregate metrics complete the toolkit. <strong>Energy return on investment
    (EROI)</strong> — energy delivered per unit of energy invested — is the
    net-energy lens the energy chapter's transitions depend on: historical oil
    delivered ~30–100× (and still does at the best fields), while solar PV's
    energy payback time is now ~1–3 years against a 25–30-year life (EROI of
    order 10–30, rising as panels improve), wind similar or better — the
    transition's central arithmetic is that renewables' EROI now clears the
    civilisational bar (~5–10) with room to spare, though system integration
    (storage, grid) still eats into it. <strong>Circularity</strong> is measured
    by how much material input comes from cycled sources — and the headline is
    sobering: the global economy is only ~7% circular (the Circularity Gap
    reports), with the rest extracted, used once and lost. The LCA discipline
    supplies the design loop that would raise it: design for disassembly,
    material passports, and the recycling hierarchy (reduce, reuse, recycle —
    in that order, because each step down the hierarchy pays an energy and
    quality toll; aluminium's 95%-energy-saving remelt is the best case, mixed
    plastics the worst). The energy arithmetic of recycling, concretely: remelting
    aluminium takes ~5% of smelting from bauxite; steel ~25–30%; glass ~60–70% (the
    melting energy dominates either way); paper saves ~40% but costs fibre length
    each cycle (5–7 loops before virgin fibre must re-enter). Recycling is not a
    virtue in itself — it is an energy and materials trade measured like any other,
    and the hierarchy exists because the trades rank that way.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a high LCA number is self-interpreting — "plastic
    bags are bad, so ban them." LCA produces <em>comparative</em> results with
    boundaries, and the policy layer must add the value weighting: a bag ban might
    win on litter and wildlife while losing marginally on carbon (people reuse
    bin liners). The mature use of LCA is as one input to a decision, with its
    uncertainty bands and its boundary choices stated — never as a verdict machine.
    A companion rule for reading claims: <strong>ask for the functional unit</strong>.
    "Our new bottle is 30% better" — better per bottle, per litre, per year of
    service? Rebound effects hide in denominators: a lighter car driven farther can
    lose its manufacturing gains entirely. Every published LCA states its functional
    unit in the first paragraph; every greenwash avoids one somewhere in the small
    print.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Attributional vs consequential LCA.</strong> The standard method
    (attributional) averages the supply chain as it is; the consequential variant asks
    what <em>changes</em> if demand for the product changes — building the marginal
    supplier (often the dirtiest, most flexible producer) into the answer. The two
    give different verdicts on the same product: consequential LCA typically punishes
    added beef demand more than attributional (marginal beef is deforestation-linked),
    and favours renewables less (marginal electricity is often gas). Policy LCA is
    consequential by necessity; eco-labels are attributional by practicality — the
    distinction explains many cross-study "contradictions" that are actually answers
    to different questions. Related: <strong>system expansion</strong> (crediting
    co-products) and <strong>allocation</strong> (how to split a dairy cow's footprint
    between milk and meat) are the technical knots where LCAs most honestly disagree.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Uncertainty, temporal dynamics and context.</strong> Good practice
    propagates uncertainty (Monte Carlo over the inventory — the statistics lesson's
    machinery) and reports which parameters dominate. Newer refinements: dynamic LCA
    (timing matters — a tonne emitted during peak warming costs more than one
    absorbed now), <strong>GWP*</strong> (a metric pair that treats short-lived gases
    as flow, not stock — resolving part of the livestock-accounting controversy),
    regionalised characterisation (a water-stress factor in Spain is not one in
    Norway), and social LCA (labour, health, community — same architecture, harder
    data). The regulatory frontier industrialises all of it: the EU's digital
    product passports and carbon border adjustments (CBAM) effectively make LCA a
    customs document, which is forcing data quality on supply chains the way
    financial audit forced accounting on firms.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> The field's live problems: how to measure
    circularity honestly (recycled content is easy to game; "recyclable" is a design
    claim, not an outcome — the circularity-gap number hides quality loss downcycling);
    how to weight planetary-boundary exceedance into product metrics (Richardson et
    al., 2023, found six of nine boundaries crossed and argued Earth-system context
    belongs in LCA's denominators); whether absolute decoupling is achievable at
    global scale fast enough (the degrowth-versus-green-growth debate turns on this
    measurement); and how to keep disclosure from becoming box-ticking — the
    assurance problem, since metrics that pay for themselves get audited, and metrics
    that merely flatter do not. The common thread: sustainability measurement is
    young, contested at the edges, and — unlike the physics beneath it — still
    partly a governance choice.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ISO 14040/14044 LCA standards; Poore &amp; Nemecek, <em>Science</em> (2018); Richardson et al., <em>Science Advances</em> (2023); GHG Protocol; UK Environment Agency bag LCA (2011); Circle Economy, Circularity Gap Report.</p>
</TopicPage>
